<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">British colonization of the Americas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">British colonization of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> began in the late 16th century with failed English attempts such as the short-lived Roanoke Colony, established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Sir Walter Raleigh</a> in 1585, and the first permanent English colony was established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, in 1607. Colonies followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, among them Massachusetts in 1620, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and Rupert's Land. In 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Floridas</a>, but many North American colonies won independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, which ended in 1783. Most remaining colonies gained independence between 1962 and 1983, while some remain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territories</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the first voyage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> in 1492, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal established colonies in the New World. In 1497 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII of England</a> dispatched an expedition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cabot">John Cabot</a> to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, but the lack of precious metals or other riches discouraged both the Spanish and the English from settling there permanently. Later explorers such as Martin Frobisher and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson">Henry Hudson</a> sought a Northwest Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and found no viable route. Europeans fished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-banks-of-newfoundland">Grand Banks of Newfoundland</a> and traded metal, glass and cloth for food and fur, which began the North American fur trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1606 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> granted charters to the Plymouth Company and the London Company. The London Company founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a> in 1607, while the Plymouth Company's Popham Colony proved short-lived. About 30,000 Algonquian people lived in the region. By 1617 only 351 of the 1,700 colonists sent to Jamestown were still alive. Tobacco then raised the settlement from 400 settlers in 1617 to 1,240 in 1622. Frequent war with American Indians helped bankrupt the London Company, and the crown took direct control of the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>.</p><p>In 1629 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a> founded the Massachusetts Bay Colony, and by 1635 roughly ten thousand English settlers lived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennebec-river">Kennebec River</a>. Roger Williams, expelled from Massachusetts Bay, founded Rhode Island and Providence Plantations. The success of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> encouraged other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> sugar colonies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1655. In 1670 Charles II chartered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>, giving it a monopoly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> of Rupert's Land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After decades of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Floridas</a> in 1763. Many of the Thirteen Colonies won independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, which ended in 1783. Historians call the empire after 1783 the Second British Empire, which looked increasingly to Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and to trade rather than territory.</p><p>Most North American colonies joined the Confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in the 1860s or 1870s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> followed in 1949. Canada gained full autonomy through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-westminster-1931">Statute of Westminster 1931</a>. Most remaining colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> became independent between 1962 and 1983, a process that was relatively peaceful in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Anguilla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a>, the British Virgin Islands, the Cayman Islands, the Falkland Islands, Montserrat and the Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caicos-islands">Caicos Islands</a> remain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territories</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “British colonization of the Americas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
